bout reliable, affordable access to a capability experts can partly assemble today, and a domain-dependent bottleneck: where consequences are already computable, verification or execution reliability may bind instead. Built, it is a typed interface plus a loop: a frontier model proposes interventions and hypotheses and explains outcomes; a decision-class model prunes, scores, and gates; the world model rolls state forward with uncertainty; the grounding loop chooses the experiments that tighten it. A user could then make a design tool for a product, process, policy, or physical system that has never been modelled, that returns an outcome distribution with its validity range before anything is built and names the experiment that would narrow it; an agent that searches plans inside the model and acts only when simulated success clears a bar; and authored simulations specified by constraints and examples whose behaviour stays consistent under interventions no one wrote by hand.
